AI Clinical Trial Card: Generative AI without Guardrails Can Harm Learning: Evidence from High School Mathematics

Authors: Hamsa Bastani, Osbert Bastani, Alp Sungu, Haosen Ge, Ozge Kabakcı, Rei Mariman

AI Model Details

Provider: OpenAI

Model Name and Version:

GPT-4 (exact version not specified)

Access Method:

Chat-based AI tutoring system

Fine-tuning:

No fine-tuning reported

Prompting / Configuration Parameters:

Two AI conditions:

Without guardrails: AI freely answered questions

With guardrails: AI constrained to provide hints and guidance rather than full solutions

Human Participant Profile

Sample Size:

Approximately 1,000 high school students

Population / Expertise:

Students with limited prior mastery of the material

Domain Expertise:

High school mathematics

AI / LLM Familiarity:

Low

Training / Onboarding:

Minimal instructions

Interaction and Task Design

Task Description:

Students worked through math problems using an AI tutor

AI Role / Degree of Autonomy:

Assistive, but with varying levels of constraint

Experimental Conditions:

No AI

AI without guardrails

AI with pedagogical guardrails

Primary Outcome Measures:

Test scores and learning gains

Main Effects:

Unguarded AI reduced learning outcomes, likely due to over-reliance

Guardrailed AI mitigated harms and partially restored performance

Notes on Interpretation

Shows that interaction design, not just model capability, is crucial

Highlights risks of deploying generative AI in learning contexts without pedagogical constraints

Strong contrast with productivity-focused studies